{
  "concept": "As the incident angle inside the denser medium grows, the refracted ray bends farther from the normal until, at the critical angle where sin(theta_c) = n2/n1, it grazes the surface and beyond that angle no light escapes — all of it is totally internally reflected.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Horizontal boundary line across the middle of the canvas separating two media",
    "Lower half filled with a shaded blue-gray gradient rectangle representing the denser medium, labeled 'n₁' at its right edge",
    "Upper half left unshaded (white), labeled 'n₂' at its upper left",
    "Vertical black dashed normal line through the point of incidence, extending above and below the boundary",
    "Point of incidence marked where all rays meet on the boundary",
    "Incident ray: solid teal line rising from lower left to the point of incidence, with a mid-line arrowhead pointing up-right, labeled 'Incident ray'",
    "Reflected ray: solid teal line leaving the point of incidence down-right into n₁, with arrowhead, labeled 'Reflected ray'",
    "Refracted ray: solid teal line leaving the point of incidence up-right into n₂, with arrowhead, labeled 'Refracted ray' (hidden when total internal reflection occurs)",
    "Angle arc and label 'θ₁' between the normal (below boundary, left side) and the incident ray",
    "Angle arc and label 'θ₁' between the normal (below boundary, right side) and the reflected ray",
    "Angle arc and label 'θ₂' between the normal (above boundary) and the refracted ray",
    "Small right-angle square symbol at the boundary with label 'θ₂ = 90°' shown when the refracted ray grazes the interface",
    "Dashed teal reference ray drawn at the critical angle inside n₁, with angle arc labeled 'θ_c'",
    "Annotation text 'Total internal reflection' in the lower right region when θ₁ > θ_c",
    "Readout panel showing current θ₁, computed θ₂ (or 'no refracted ray'), n₂/n₁, and θ_c",
    "Equation annotations: 'n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂' and 'sin θ_c = n₂/n₁'",
    "Panel captions (a) partial refraction, (b) grazing at critical angle, (c) total internal reflection, highlighted according to current regime"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "incidentAngle",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Increasing θ₁ inside the denser medium swings the refracted ray farther from the normal until it lies flat along the surface and then vanishes entirely, while the reflected ray persists and grows brighter.",
      "label": "Incident angle θ₁ (degrees)",
      "range": [0, 89, 1],
      "default": 25
    },
    {
      "id": "indexRatio",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "The critical angle is set entirely by the index ratio: lowering n₂/n₁ pulls θ_c toward the normal so total internal reflection starts at shallower incidence, making sin θ_c = n₂/n₁ visible as the boundary between escape and confinement.",
      "label": "Index ratio n₂/n₁",
      "range": [0.3, 0.98, 0.01],
      "default": 0.67
    },
    {
      "id": "showCriticalRef",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "Comparing the live incident ray with a fixed dashed ray at θ_c shows that the only thing that matters is whether θ₁ sits inside or outside that reference angle.",
      "label": "Show θ_c reference ray",
      "default": true
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Partial refraction (panel a)",
      "narration": "At a modest incident angle the ray splits: part refracts into the less dense medium, bending away from the normal (θ₂ > θ₁), and part reflects back at exactly θ₁.",
      "state": { "incidentAngle": 25, "indexRatio": 0.67, "showCriticalRef": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Pushing θ₂ toward 90°",
      "narration": "Raise θ₁ and watch the refracted ray swing outward much faster than the incident ray tilts — it is racing toward the surface.",
      "state": { "incidentAngle": 38, "indexRatio": 0.67, "showCriticalRef": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "The critical angle (panel b)",
      "narration": "At θ₁ = θ_c ≈ 42° the refracted ray lies flat along the interface: θ₂ = 90°. This is the last angle at which any light escapes.",
      "state": { "incidentAngle": 42, "indexRatio": 0.67, "showCriticalRef": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Total internal reflection (panel c)",
      "narration": "Go just a degree past θ_c and the refracted ray disappears — Snell's law has no solution. All the light stays inside n₁ as a single reflected ray.",
      "state": { "incidentAngle": 55, "indexRatio": 0.67, "showCriticalRef": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Change the media",
      "narration": "Now hold θ₁ fixed and shrink n₂/n₁. The dashed θ_c reference swings toward the normal, and the same ray that was escaping is suddenly trapped: sin θ_c = n₂/n₁.",
      "state": { "incidentAngle": 40, "indexRatio": 0.45, "showCriticalRef": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Nearly matched media",
      "narration": "Push n₂/n₁ close to 1 and θ_c approaches 90° — with almost identical media, light escapes at nearly every angle and total internal reflection all but vanishes.",
      "state": { "incidentAngle": 40, "indexRatio": 0.96, "showCriticalRef": true }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Total internal reflection is not a new law but the point where Snell's law runs out of room: once sin θ₁ exceeds n₂/n₁ there is no angle θ₂ that satisfies it, so the transmitted ray simply cannot exist.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Single wide interactive diagram replacing the original three panels, with the (a)/(b)/(c) cases becoming regimes of one slider. Lower half of the canvas is a light blue-to-gray gradient rectangle (denser medium) labeled n₁ at the right edge; upper half white, labeled n₂ at the upper left; a thin blue boundary line between them. A black vertical dashed normal passes through the incidence point at the horizontal center. All rays are solid teal (#1a7f92-ish) lines of equal weight with a single arrowhead at the midpoint indicating propagation direction: incident ray up-right from lower left, reflected ray down-right, refracted ray up-right above the boundary. Angle arcs are thin teal arcs with italic serif labels θ₁ (twice, mirrored about the normal below the boundary), θ₂ (above boundary), and a dashed teal critical-angle reference ray with its own θ_c arc. When θ₂ reaches 90° draw the small right-angle square at the interface and the label 'θ₂ = 90°'; when θ₁ > θ_c hide the refracted ray and print 'Total internal reflection' in the lower-right of the shaded region. A small readout box in a corner lists θ₁, θ₂ (or '—'), n₂/n₁, and θ_c, with the equations n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂ and sin θ_c = n₂/n₁; highlight the equation term that breaks down (sin θ₂ > 1) in the total-internal-reflection regime. Optionally scale reflected-ray opacity upward as θ₁ approaches and passes θ_c to hint at energy redistribution."
}

